/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cka.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cka", [["cka1",0,1,"An etcd backup on a kubeadm control plane node hangs and then fails with \"context deadline exceeded\":\n\netcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 snapshot save /opt/etcd.db\n\nWhat is missing?",["A --data-dir flag pointing at /var/lib/etcd on the node","A --kubeconfig flag pointing at /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf","The --cacert, --cert and --key flags pointing at the etcd PKI files","Stopping kube-apiserver first so etcd is quiet while the snapshot is taken"],2,"kubeadm's etcd only accepts mutual-TLS clients, so etcdctl needs --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt plus a client cert and key (server.crt/server.key or healthcheck-client). etcdctl does not use a kubeconfig, and snapshots are taken online without stopping the API server.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters"],["cka2",0,1,"You restored a snapshot with:\n\netcdutl snapshot restore /opt/etcd.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restore\n\nThe cluster still shows the old state. What is the next step on a kubeadm cluster?",["Point the etcd-data hostPath in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml at /var/lib/etcd-restore","Run kubectl apply -f /var/lib/etcd-restore on the control plane so the API server loads every restored object","Restart kubelet with --etcd-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restore on every node","Run kubeadm upgrade apply so the API server reads the new data directory"],0,"Restoring only writes a new data directory. The etcd static Pod must be told to use it by changing the hostPath volume (or --data-dir) in its manifest; kubelet then recreates etcd. kubectl cannot read raw etcd data, and kubelet has no etcd flag.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters"],["cka3",0,1,"You are upgrading the first control plane node of a kubeadm cluster from 1.34.x to 1.35.x. After upgrading the kubeadm package, which order is correct?",["Upgrade kubelet; restart kubelet; kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x; kubeadm upgrade plan; uncordon","kubeadm upgrade node; drain; upgrade kubelet; kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x; uncordon","Drain every node at once; run kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x on each node in turn; then upgrade kubelet on the workers last","kubeadm upgrade plan; kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x; drain; upgrade kubelet and kubectl; restart kubelet; uncordon"],3,"The documented flow is: new kubeadm, plan, apply (upgrades the control plane static Pods), then drain, upgrade and restart kubelet/kubectl, and uncordon. Upgrading kubelet before the control plane would make it newer than the API server, which the version skew policy does not allow.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters"],["cka4",0,1,"The first control plane node is already on 1.35. Which kubeadm command upgrades the other control plane nodes and the worker nodes?",["kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x","kubeadm upgrade node","kubeadm join --upgrade","kubeadm init phase upgrade"],1,"kubeadm upgrade apply is run once, on the first control plane node. Every other node runs kubeadm upgrade node, which upgrades its local control plane components (if any) and kubelet configuration.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters"],["cka5",0,1,"A cluster runs Kubernetes 1.33. The team wants to get to 1.35 this weekend with kubeadm. What is the supported path?",["Run kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x directly on 1.33","Upgrade to 1.34 first, then from 1.34 to 1.35","Upgrade the workers to 1.35 first, then the control plane","Rebuild the control plane on 1.35 and join the old workers"],1,"kubeadm supports one minor version per upgrade, so 1.33 to 1.35 must go through 1.34. Workers must never be newer than the API server, so the control plane is always upgraded first.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters"],["cka6",0,1,"A new worker must join the cluster, but the bootstrap token from kubeadm init expired days ago. Which command on the control plane gives you a working join command?",["kubeadm init phase bootstrap-token --renew","kubectl create token kubeadm --duration=24h","kubeadm certs renew bootstrap --print-join","kubeadm token create --print-join-command"],3,"kubeadm token create makes a new bootstrap token, and --print-join-command prints the full kubeadm join line with the CA cert hash. kubectl create token issues ServiceAccount tokens, not bootstrap tokens.","Create and manage Kubernetes clusters using kubeadm"],["cka7",0,1,"kubeadm init stops at preflight with:\n\n[ERROR FileContent--proc-sys-net-ipv4-ip_forward]: /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward contents are not set to 1\n\nWhat is the right fix?",["Set net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1 in /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf and run sysctl --system","Load the ip_forward kernel module with modprobe, then run kubeadm reset and start again","Add --ignore-preflight-errors=all to kubeadm init and continue","Disable firewalld so packets are forwarded between interfaces"],0,"IP forwarding is a kernel sysctl, not a module. Persisting it in /etc/sysctl.d and applying with sysctl --system fixes the check properly; skipping preflight hides a setting Pod networking really needs.","Prepare underlying infrastructure for installing a Kubernetes cluster"],["cka8",0,1,"Nodes installed with kubeadm show Pods restarting randomly. kubelet uses cgroupDriver: systemd, but containerd's config.toml has SystemdCgroup = false. What should you do?",["Change the kubelet cgroupDriver to cgroupfs in /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml to match the runtime","Remove the cgroup settings from both files and let them negotiate","Set SystemdCgroup = true in the containerd runc options and restart containerd","Reinstall containerd from the distro package to reset config.toml"],2,"kubelet and the container runtime must use the same cgroup driver. On systemd hosts the recommended driver is systemd for both, which kubeadm already sets for kubelet, so the runtime side is what needs changing. They do not negotiate automatically.","Prepare underlying infrastructure for installing a Kubernetes cluster"],["cka9",0,1,"While preparing Ubuntu VMs for kubeadm, which pair of kernel modules is normally loaded (and persisted in /etc/modules-load.d) for containerd and bridged Pod traffic?",["ip_vs and nf_nat_ftp","vxlan and 8021q","bonding and bridge_ipv6","overlay and br_netfilter"],3,"overlay backs containerd's overlayfs snapshotter and br_netfilter lets iptables see bridged traffic (needed for net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables). ip_vs is only needed for kube-proxy IPVS mode.","Prepare underlying infrastructure for installing a Kubernetes cluster"],["cka10",0,1,"You plan a highly available kubeadm control plane behind a load balancer at k8s-api.lab.local:6443. Which kubeadm init option makes every kubeconfig and certificate use that address?",["--apiserver-advertise-address=k8s-api.lab.local","--apiserver-bind-port=6443 --node-name=k8s-api","--control-plane-endpoint=k8s-api.lab.local:6443","--service-dns-domain=k8s-api.lab.local"],2,"--control-plane-endpoint sets the shared endpoint that nodes and kubeconfigs use, and it cannot easily be added later. --apiserver-advertise-address is this node's own IP, not the load-balanced name.","Implement and configure a highly-available control plane"],["cka11",0,1,"You want to add a second control plane node with kubeadm join. Which extra flags does the join command need so the node gets the shared certificates automatically?",["--apiserver-advertise-address <lb-ip> --token <token>","--control-plane --certificate-key <key>","--upload-certs --discovery-token-unsafe-skip-ca-verification","--node-role control-plane --cri-socket <socket>"],1,"The key comes from kubeadm init --upload-certs (or kubeadm init phase upload-certs --upload-certs), which stores the encrypted certs in a Secret; --control-plane with --certificate-key downloads and decrypts them. --upload-certs is an init flag, not a join flag.","Implement and configure a highly-available control plane"],["cka12",0,1,"A stacked-etcd control plane has three control plane nodes. How many of them can fail while the cluster can still write to etcd?",["One","None","Two","Three"],0,"etcd needs a majority (quorum): 2 of 3. With one node down the other two still form a quorum; losing two leaves one member, which cannot write. Five members tolerate two failures.","Implement and configure a highly-available control plane"],["cka13",0,1,"What is the key difference between the stacked etcd and external etcd HA topologies in kubeadm?",["Stacked stores data in the API server's memory; external stores it on disk","Stacked needs no load balancer in front of the API servers, while external etcd always needs one","Stacked runs an etcd member on each control plane node; external runs etcd on separate hosts","Stacked uses one etcd member for all nodes; external uses one member per worker"],2,"Stacked is simpler and needs fewer machines but couples etcd and control plane failures. External etcd needs more hosts but isolates them. Both need a load balancer for the API servers.","Implement and configure a highly-available control plane"],["cka14",0,1,"kubectl suddenly fails with \"x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid\" on a year-old kubeadm cluster. Which command shows which certificates are affected?",["kubeadm certs check-expiration","kubectl get csr --all-namespaces","openssl x509 -checkend /etc/kubernetes","kubeadm token list --expired"],0,"kubeadm certs check-expiration lists every kubeadm-managed certificate and its expiry. You then run kubeadm certs renew all and restart the control plane static Pods. CSRs are requests, not issued certificates.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters"],["cka15",0,1,"Where does kubelet on a kubeadm control plane node look for the kube-apiserver, etcd, scheduler and controller-manager manifests?",["/var/lib/etcd/manifests, set by the etcd --data-dir flag","/etc/kubernetes/pki, next to the cluster certificates","/opt/cni/bin, set by the CNI plugin configuration","/etc/kubernetes/manifests, set by staticPodPath in the kubelet config"],3,"Control plane components on kubeadm clusters are static Pods. kubelet watches staticPodPath (default /etc/kubernetes/manifests) and runs whatever manifests it finds there; editing a file there restarts that component.","Create and manage Kubernetes clusters using kubeadm"],["cka16",0,1,"Group ops needs read-only access to every namespace, including new ones. The built-in ClusterRole view already has the right rules. Which command grants it?",["kubectl create rolebinding ops-view --clusterrole=view --group=ops -A","kubectl create clusterrolebinding ops-view --clusterrole=view --group=ops","kubectl create clusterrole ops-view --verb=get,list --resource=* --group=ops","kubectl auth reconcile --clusterrole=view --group=ops --all-namespaces"],1,"A ClusterRoleBinding applies a ClusterRole in every namespace, now and in future. A RoleBinding only works in the one namespace it is created in, and there is no -A flag to create it everywhere.","Manage role based access control (RBAC)"],["cka17",0,1,"Team leads should have the rules of ClusterRole app-admin, but only inside namespace payments. What is the least-privilege binding?",["A ClusterRoleBinding that references ClusterRole app-admin","A RoleBinding in payments that references ClusterRole app-admin","A Role in payments that references ClusterRole app-admin","A ClusterRoleBinding with namespace: payments set in its metadata"],1,"A RoleBinding can point at a ClusterRole; the rules then apply only in the RoleBinding's namespace. ClusterRoleBindings are cluster-wide and have no namespace to limit them. Roles hold rules and cannot reference other roles.","Manage role based access control (RBAC)"],["cka18",0,1,"An on-call engineer needs to list nodes. You create a Role with verbs get and list on nodes and a RoleBinding in default, but access is still denied. Why?",["The Role must also grant the watch verb before list works","RoleBindings only apply to ServiceAccounts, not to human users","The Role must be created in kube-system, because nodes are system objects owned by that namespace","Nodes are cluster-scoped, so the rule must be in a ClusterRole bound with a ClusterRoleBinding"],3,"Rules for cluster-scoped resources (nodes, PersistentVolumes, namespaces) only take effect through a ClusterRole and ClusterRoleBinding. list does not depend on watch, and RoleBindings work for users, groups and ServiceAccounts.","Manage role based access control (RBAC)"],["cka19",0,1,"User jane submitted a CertificateSigningRequest named jane with signerName kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client. What must an admin do so she gets a client certificate?",["kubectl certificate approve jane","kubectl create clusterrolebinding jane --user=jane","kubeadm certs renew jane --csr-dir=/tmp","kubectl sign csr jane --ca=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt"],0,"Approving the CSR lets the controller manager sign it; the cert is then in .status.certificate. A binding only gives permissions after she can authenticate, so it is a separate, later step.","Manage role based access control (RBAC)"],["cka20",0,1,"Before a kernel patch on worker node01 you must move its workloads off. The node runs DaemonSet Pods and some Pods using emptyDir. Which command works?",["kubectl cordon node01 --evict-all --force-daemonsets","kubectl delete node node01 --grace-period=60","kubectl drain node01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data","kubectl taint node node01 maintenance=true:NoSchedule --drain=true"],2,"drain cordons the node and evicts Pods. It refuses to continue with DaemonSet Pods or emptyDir data unless you pass those two flags. cordon alone only stops new scheduling, and deleting the node object does not stop its Pods cleanly.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters"],["cka21",0,1,"kubectl drain node02 keeps retrying with \"Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod's disruption budget\". What is happening?",["The Pod carries a toleration for the node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable taint that drain applies","The node has a NoExecute taint that blocks the Eviction API","kubelet on node02 is down, so evictions cannot be confirmed","A PodDisruptionBudget's minAvailable would be broken if this Pod were evicted now"],3,"Eviction honours PodDisruptionBudgets. If evicting would drop the app below minAvailable (for example because other replicas are not Ready elsewhere), drain waits. Scale up or fix the other replicas rather than deleting the PDB.","Manage the lifecycle of Kubernetes clusters"],["cka22",0,1,"Which statement matches the extension interfaces in Kubernetes?",["CRI configures cluster DNS, CNI connects kubelet to containerd, CSI stores Secrets","CRI is the API server's storage layer, CNI handles Ingress, CSI runs Pod sandboxes","CRI connects kubelet to the runtime, CNI sets up Pod networking, CSI provisions and mounts storage","CRI signs node certificates, CNI enforces RBAC for kubelets, CSI implements Service load balancing"],2,"kubelet talks gRPC to a CRI runtime such as containerd or CRI-O; a CNI plugin (Calico, Cilium, Flannel) gives Pods IPs and routes; a CSI driver lets storage vendors plug in provisioning and attach/mount.","Understand extension interfaces (CNI, CSI, CRI, etc.)"],["cka23",0,1,"You applied an operator's CRDs and created a Database custom resource. kubectl get databases shows it, but no Pods or Services ever appear. What is the most likely cause?",["The CRD was created with scope: Namespaced when it should be Cluster-scoped","The operator's controller Deployment is not installed or not running","Custom resources need a ValidatingWebhook before they are stored","kubectl apply must be run with --server-side for custom resources"],1,"A CRD only teaches the API server a new type; the operator's controller watches those objects and creates the real resources. If the object is stored and listed, the API side works and the missing piece is the controller.","Understand CRDs, install and configure operators"],["cka24",0,1,"You need to install the cert-manager chart at chart version 1.16.0 and make the command safe to rerun in a script. Which command fits?",["helm upgrade --install cert-manager jetstack/cert-manager -n cert-manager --create-namespace --version 1.16.0","helm install cert-manager jetstack/cert-manager --app-version 1.16.0 --replace -n cert-manager","helm pull jetstack/cert-manager --version 1.16.0 && kubectl apply -f cert-manager-1.16.0.tgz -n cert-manager --force","helm install jetstack/cert-manager --name cert-manager --tag v1.16.0 --namespace cert-manager"],0,"upgrade --install installs when the release is missing and upgrades when it exists, and --version pins the chart version. --app-version is not an install flag, a .tgz chart cannot be applied with kubectl, and --name was removed in Helm 3.","Use Helm and Kustomize to install cluster components"],["cka25",0,2,"Only GPU workloads may be scheduled on node gpu1. Which command keeps other Pods off it without evicting what is already running?",["kubectl taint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu:NoExecute","kubectl label nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu --overwrite","kubectl taint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule","kubectl cordon gpu1 --selector dedicated=gpu"],2,"NoSchedule stops new Pods without a matching toleration but leaves running Pods alone. NoExecute would also evict running Pods that do not tolerate it. A label only attracts Pods that ask for it; it never repels others.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)"],["cka26",0,2,"Which command removes the taint dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule from node gpu1?",["kubectl taint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule-","kubectl untaint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu","kubectl taint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu:None","kubectl delete taint gpu1/dedicated"],0,"A trailing minus on the taint (or just on the key, dedicated-) removes it. There is no untaint or delete taint command.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)"],["cka27",0,2,"A Pod has this toleration:\n\ntolerations:\n- key: dedicated\n  operator: Equal\n  value: gpu\n  effect: NoSchedule\n\nNode gpu1 is tainted dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule. Where can the Pod run?",["Only on gpu1, because the toleration pins the Pod to tainted nodes","Nowhere yet: a nodeSelector for dedicated=gpu must also be added to the Pod spec first","Only on nodes that have no taints at all, since values must be empty","On gpu1 or on any untainted node; a toleration allows but does not require gpu1"],3,"Tolerations remove a barrier; they do not attract. To force the Pod onto gpu1 you also need a nodeSelector or node affinity on a gpu label. The usual dedicated-node pattern is taint plus label plus toleration plus affinity.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)"],["cka28",0,2,"Read this Pod snippet:\n\naffinity:\n  nodeAffinity:\n    requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:\n      nodeSelectorTerms:\n      - matchExpressions:\n        - key: disktype\n          operator: In\n          values: [ssd]\n\nNo node is labeled disktype=ssd. What happens?",["The Pod is scheduled anywhere because no node matches","The Pod stays Pending until a node gets the label","The Pod is scheduled on the node with the most free disk","The API server rejects the Pod because no node matches"],1,"required... is a hard rule: with no matching node the scheduler leaves the Pod Pending. preferredDuringScheduling... would be the soft version that falls back to any node. IgnoredDuringExecution means removing the label later does not evict running Pods.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)"],["cka29",0,2,"The kube-scheduler is down. Which new Pod can still start?",["A Pod with a nodeSelector for kubernetes.io/hostname=worker1","A Pod whose spec sets nodeName: worker1","A Pod with a toleration for every taint","A Pod created by a Deployment with one replica"],1,"Setting spec.nodeName skips scheduling entirely: kubelet on that node sees the Pod bound to it and runs it. nodeSelector, tolerations and Deployments all still need the scheduler to bind the Pod.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)"],["cka30",0,2,"A 3-replica Deployment must never have two replicas on the same node. Which setting enforces that?",["podAffinity preferred, matching the app label, with topologyKey topology.kubernetes.io/zone","nodeAffinity required on kubernetes.io/hostname with operator NotIn","A PodDisruptionBudget with maxUnavailable: 1 on the app label","podAntiAffinity required, matching the app label, with topologyKey kubernetes.io/hostname"],3,"Required Pod anti-affinity against Pods with the same label, per hostname, puts at most one replica on each node. Pod affinity does the opposite (co-location), and a PDB limits voluntary disruptions, not placement.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)"],["cka31",0,2,"Read this snippet from a Deployment Pod template:\n\ntopologySpreadConstraints:\n- maxSkew: 1\n  topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone\n  whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule\n  labelSelector:\n    matchLabels: {app: web}\n\nWhat does it do?",["Keeps the web Pod count per zone within one of each other, leaving Pods Pending if needed","Allows at most one web Pod in each zone and has the API server reject any extra Pods at admission","Prefers zones with fewer Pods but schedules anywhere if zones are uneven","Moves running web Pods between zones whenever the counts differ"],0,"maxSkew 1 means the busiest and quietest zones may differ by at most one matching Pod. DoNotSchedule makes it a hard rule (ScheduleAnyway would make it soft). The scheduler never moves running Pods to rebalance.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)"],["cka32",0,2,"Which command creates a HorizontalPodAutoscaler that keeps Deployment web between 2 and 10 replicas at about 70% CPU?",["kubectl scale deployment web --min=2 --max=10 --cpu=70%","kubectl create hpa web --target-cpu=70 --replicas=2-10","kubectl autoscale deployment web --cpu-percent=70 --min=2 --max=10","kubectl set autoscale deployment/web cpu=70 min=2 max=10"],2,"kubectl autoscale creates an autoscaling/v2 HPA with a CPU utilization target. kubectl scale sets a fixed replica count and has no min or max.","Configure workload autoscaling"],["cka33",0,2,"kubectl get hpa web shows TARGETS <unknown>/70% and the replica count never changes. metrics-server is running and kubectl top pods works. What is the most likely cause?",["The HPA must be in kube-system to read the Metrics API","The Deployment uses the Recreate strategy, which HorizontalPodAutoscalers cannot scale","maxReplicas is lower than the current number of replicas","The web containers have no CPU requests, so utilization cannot be calculated"],3,"CPU utilization is usage divided by the request. Without requests the HPA cannot compute a percentage and shows <unknown>. Since kubectl top works, the Metrics API itself is fine.","Configure workload autoscaling"],["cka34",0,2,"A payments Pod with a high PriorityClass is Pending because the cluster is full. Several low-priority batch Pods are running. What does the scheduler do by default?",["Waits until a batch Pod finishes on its own","Adds a new node through the scheduler's autoscaler","Preempts (evicts) lower-priority Pods so the payments Pod can fit","Rejects the payments Pod at admission for lack of capacity"],2,"Higher-priority Pods can preempt lower-priority ones unless the PriorityClass sets preemptionPolicy: Never. Adding nodes is the Cluster Autoscaler's job, which is a separate component.","Configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)"],["cka35",0,2,"A node-exporter DaemonSet runs on every worker but not on the kubeadm control plane node. What needs adding to its Pod template?",["A nodeSelector for node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane=true","A toleration for node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane with effect NoSchedule","spec.nodeName set to the control plane node's name in the DaemonSet's Pod template","hostNetwork: true so it can reach the control plane"],1,"kubeadm taints control plane nodes node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule. The DaemonSet controller respects taints, so the Pods need a matching toleration. A nodeSelector for the control plane would remove it from the workers.","Understand the primitives used to create robust, self-healing, application deployments"],["cka36",0,2,"You delete one Pod of a 3-replica Deployment by name. What happens next?",["The ReplicaSet notices only 2 Pods match and creates a replacement","The Deployment scales down to 2 replicas and records a new revision","Nothing until you run kubectl rollout restart on the Deployment","kubelet restarts the same Pod object on the same node"],0,"The ReplicaSet controller keeps the number of matching Pods at the desired count, so a new Pod (with a new name) is created. That is the self-healing loop; to really remove a replica you change spec.replicas.","Understand the primitives used to create robust, self-healing, application deployments"],["cka37",0,2,"You updated Secret db-cred, which Deployment api uses through env vars. The Pod template did not change. How do you get the running Pods to pick up the new value with a rolling update?",["kubectl rollout undo deployment/api","kubectl replace --force -f secret.yaml","kubectl rollout restart deployment/api","kubectl annotate secret db-cred reload=true"],2,"Environment variables are read only when a container starts. rollout restart adds a timestamp annotation to the Pod template, which triggers a normal rolling replacement. rollout undo would go back to an older template instead.","Use ConfigMaps and Secrets to configure applications"],["cka38",0,2,"A ConfigMap was created with immutable: true. You now need to change one of its keys. What must you do?",["Delete and recreate the ConfigMap (or create a new one and point the Pods at it)","Run kubectl edit and set immutable: false, then change the key","Use kubectl patch with --force to change only the data field","Change it with kubectl apply --server-side, which skips the immutable check"],0,"Once immutable is true, neither data nor the immutable field can be changed; the only way is a new object. A common pattern is versioned names (app-cfg-v2) with a rollout. Immutable ConfigMaps and Secrets also reduce API server watch load.","Use ConfigMaps and Secrets to configure applications"],["cka39",0,3,"Which statement matches the Kubernetes network model?",["Pods share the node's IP and are told apart only by port numbers","Pods can only reach other Pods through a Service ClusterIP, never directly by Pod IP","Pod-to-Pod traffic across nodes is always NATed to the node IP","Every Pod gets its own IP, and Pods on any node can reach each other without NAT"],3,"The model requires a unique IP per Pod and flat, NAT-free Pod-to-Pod connectivity across nodes; the CNI plugin implements it. Services add stable virtual IPs on top but are not required for Pods to talk.","Understand connectivity between Pods"],["cka40",0,3,"From a debug Pod, `curl http://10.96.40.12:80` to a Service works, but `ping 10.96.40.12` gets no reply. kube-proxy runs in iptables mode. What is going on?",["The Service is broken, because a healthy ClusterIP always answers ping","Normal: a ClusterIP is virtual and only forwards the Service's ports, not ICMP","A NetworkPolicy is blocking ICMP but not TCP to the Service","CoreDNS is failing, so ping cannot resolve the address"],1,"A ClusterIP is not bound to any interface; kube-proxy rules only translate the declared protocol and port to endpoint Pods. ping is not a valid test for Services. The address is used directly, so DNS is not involved.","Understand connectivity between Pods"],["cka41",0,3,"What is kube-proxy's job on each node?",["Give each Pod an IP address from the node's Pod CIDR","Program iptables, IPVS or nftables rules that send Service traffic to endpoint Pods","Answer DNS queries for Service names inside the cluster","Proxy kubectl and kubelet requests from that node through to the kube-apiserver on port 6443"],1,"kube-proxy watches Services and EndpointSlices and writes packet-forwarding rules. IP assignment belongs to the CNI plugin and DNS to CoreDNS.","Understand connectivity between Pods"],["cka42",0,3,"On a kubeadm lab cluster on local VMs, a Service of type LoadBalancer stays at EXTERNAL-IP <pending>. Why?",["LoadBalancer Services need an Ingress resource before they get an IP","The Service needs externalTrafficPolicy: Local set before the controller assigns an IP","kube-proxy must be switched to IPVS mode for LoadBalancer Services","Nothing in the cluster implements LoadBalancer, such as a cloud controller or MetalLB"],3,"Kubernetes only records the request; a cloud controller manager or a bare-metal implementation such as MetalLB allocates the address. The Service still works as a NodePort in the meantime.","Use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints"],["cka43",0,3,"You created a NodePort Service with nodePort: 8080 and the API server rejected it. What is the reason by default?",["NodePorts must be in the range 30000–32767","NodePort must equal the Service's targetPort","Port 8080 is reserved for the kube-apiserver","Only LoadBalancer Services may set nodePort"],0,"The API server allocates node ports from --service-node-port-range, 30000–32767 by default. The node port is independent of port and targetPort.","Use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints"],["cka44",0,3,"Which command lists the endpoints behind Service web in namespace shop using the current API?",["kubectl get svc web -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.endpoints}'","kubectl describe pods -n shop --selector=service=web","kubectl get endpointslices -n shop -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web","kubectl get ep-slices web.shop --show-labels"],2,"EndpointSlices are labeled kubernetes.io/service-name with the owning Service. A Service spec holds no endpoints, and Pods are not labeled service=web automatically.","Use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints"],["cka45",0,3,"A NodePort Service must keep the real client IP visible to the Pods. Which setting does this, and what is the trade-off?",["sessionAffinity: ClientIP; each client is pinned to one node for three hours","internalTrafficPolicy: Cluster; traffic is spread evenly but adds one extra hop","type: ClusterIP with externalIPs; the Service can then no longer be reached from outside the cluster","externalTrafficPolicy: Local; nodes without a ready local Pod will not serve the traffic"],3,"With Local, kube-proxy skips the second hop and SNAT, so Pods see the client IP, but only nodes running a Pod accept the traffic. sessionAffinity pins clients to a Pod, not to a node, and does not preserve source IPs.","Use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints"],["cka46",0,3,"In the Gateway API, which resource does a platform team create to define the listeners (ports, protocols, hostnames, TLS) that app teams attach routes to?",["GatewayClass","HTTPRoute","Gateway","IngressClass"],2,"GatewayClass names the controller implementation, a Gateway is an actual listener set built from that class, and HTTPRoutes (usually owned by app teams) attach to a Gateway through parentRefs.","Use the Gateway API to manage Ingress traffic"],["cka47",0,3,"Read this HTTPRoute, created in namespace shop:\n\nspec:\n  parentRefs:\n  - name: public-gw\n    namespace: infra\n  rules:\n  - backendRefs:\n    - name: web\n      port: 80\n\nThe Gateway public-gw in infra has a listener with allowedRoutes.namespaces.from: Same. What happens?",["The route is accepted, because parentRefs names the Gateway's namespace explicitly","The route is not accepted, because the listener only allows routes from infra","Traffic reaches web in infra rather than in shop","The Gateway copies the route into infra automatically"],1,"allowedRoutes controls which namespaces may attach routes. With from: Same only routes in infra can attach, so the route's status shows it was not accepted. Changing the listener to from: All or a namespace selector fixes it.","Use the Gateway API to manage Ingress traffic"],["cka48",0,3,"You want 90% of traffic to web-v1 and 10% to web-v2 using the Gateway API. Where do you set this?",["weight on each backendRef in the HTTPRoute rule","replicas on the two Deployments in a 9:1 ratio","A canary annotation on the Gateway listener","trafficSplit in the GatewayClass parameters"],0,"HTTPRoute backendRefs take a weight, so the split is exact and independent of Pod counts. A replica ratio only approximates a split, and GatewayClass has no traffic split field.","Use the Gateway API to manage Ingress traffic"],["cka49",0,3,"On a fresh kubeadm cluster, `kubectl apply -f httproute.yaml` fails with \"no matches for kind HTTPRoute\". What is missing?",["The networking.k8s.io/v1 API group, which must be enabled on the API server","An IngressClass marked as the cluster default","The Gateway API CRDs, which are installed separately from Kubernetes","The ingress-nginx controller, which serves the HTTPRoute kind"],2,"The Gateway API is developed as CRDs outside core Kubernetes; you install its CRDs and then a controller that implements them. HTTPRoute is in gateway.networking.k8s.io, not networking.k8s.io.","Use the Gateway API to manage Ingress traffic"],["cka50",0,3,"Two ingress controllers are installed: nginx and traefik. A new Ingress without ingressClassName is ignored by both. What makes nginx pick up such Ingresses?",["Annotate the nginx IngressClass with ingressclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: \"true\"","Set spec.defaultBackend on every Ingress to the nginx controller Service","Delete the traefik IngressClass so only one class remains","Label the namespace with ingress.kubernetes.io/controller=nginx so its Ingresses go to nginx"],0,"An Ingress without a class gets the default IngressClass at creation time. Setting ingressClassName explicitly on each Ingress also works. defaultBackend is for unmatched requests, not for choosing a controller.","Know how to use Ingress controllers and Ingress resources"],["cka51",0,3,"Names under corp.example must be resolved by the company DNS server at 10.0.0.53, while everything else keeps working as before. Where do you make this change?",["Set nameserver 10.0.0.53 in /etc/resolv.conf inside every application Pod through dnsConfig","Add a Service of type ExternalName called corp.example in kube-system","Change the kubelet clusterDNS setting on every node to 10.0.0.53","Add a corp.example block with forward . 10.0.0.53 to the Corefile in the coredns ConfigMap"],3,"CoreDNS reads its Corefile from the coredns ConfigMap; a separate server block for corp.example forwards just that zone (the reload plugin picks it up). Changing clusterDNS would send all lookups, including cluster names, to the corporate server.","Understand and use CoreDNS"],["cka52",0,3,"Inside a Pod, /etc/resolv.conf shows nameserver 10.96.0.10 and a search line with default.svc.cluster.local. Where does 10.96.0.10 come from?",["The node's own /etc/resolv.conf, copied into the Pod","kubelet's clusterDNS setting, which points at the kube-dns Service ClusterIP","The CNI plugin, which runs a small DNS server on every node and writes its address","The CoreDNS Pod's IP, written by the scheduler"],1,"With the default dnsPolicy ClusterFirst, kubelet writes clusterDNS (the kube-dns Service IP, backed by CoreDNS) into the Pod's resolv.conf. The node's resolv.conf is used only with dnsPolicy Default.","Understand and use CoreDNS"],["cka53",0,3,"A Pod runs with hostNetwork: true and can no longer resolve Service names like api.backend. What fixes it?",["Set dnsPolicy: Default","Set dnsPolicy: ClusterFirstWithHostNet","Remove the search line with a dnsConfig","Add a hostAliases entry for the kube-dns Service"],1,"Host-network Pods fall back to the node's DNS settings. ClusterFirstWithHostNet tells kubelet to use cluster DNS for them anyway. Default is exactly the node-DNS behaviour that causes the problem.","Understand and use CoreDNS"],["cka54",0,3,"A NetworkPolicy must allow ingress to db Pods only from namespace analytics. Nobody has added custom labels to namespaces. Which selector works?",["namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {name: analytics}}","podSelector: {matchLabels: {namespace: analytics}}","namespaceSelector: {matchNames: [analytics]}","namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: analytics}}"],3,"Every namespace automatically has the immutable label kubernetes.io/metadata.name set to its name. A name label only exists if someone adds it, and NetworkPolicy has no matchNames field.","Define and enforce Network Policies"],["cka55",0,3,"Read this egress rule from a NetworkPolicy:\n\negress:\n- to:\n  - ipBlock:\n      cidr: 10.20.0.0/16\n      except: [10.20.5.0/24]\n  ports:\n  - protocol: TCP\n    port: 5432\n\nWhat does it allow?",["TCP 5432 to 10.20.0.0/16 except the 10.20.5.0/24 range","All traffic to 10.20.5.0/24 and TCP 5432 elsewhere","TCP 5432 only to 10.20.5.0/24","Everything to 10.20.0.0/16 on any port except 5432"],0,"ipBlock allows the CIDR minus the except ranges, and the ports list limits it to TCP 5432. If this is the only egress rule for a policy with policyTypes Egress, DNS and everything else are blocked.","Define and enforce Network Policies"],["cka56",0,3,"A developer asks for a Service that sends each client back to the same Pod for the life of a session, with no Ingress in front. Which Service field provides this?",["externalTrafficPolicy: Local","publishNotReadyAddresses: true","sessionAffinity: ClientIP","ipFamilyPolicy: SingleStack"],2,"sessionAffinity: ClientIP makes kube-proxy send a client IP to the same endpoint (for up to three hours by default). externalTrafficPolicy controls node-level routing and source IP, not Pod stickiness.","Use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints"],["cka57",0,4,"A PV has persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain. Its PVC was deleted, and a new PVC with identical settings stays Pending. The PV shows STATUS Released. Why?",["Retain volumes can only be bound again by a PVC with the same name, in a different namespace","The PV needs a StorageClass before it can be bound a second time","The kubelet is still scrubbing the data, so binding is delayed","A Released PV keeps its old claimRef; an admin must clean it up before it can bind again"],3,"Retain keeps the data and the PV but leaves it Released with the old claimRef. The admin decides what to do with the data, then removes spec.claimRef (or recreates the PV) to make it Available again.","Configure volume types, access modes and reclaim policies"],["cka58",0,4,"What happens by default to a dynamically provisioned PV when its PVC is deleted?",["It becomes Released and keeps its data on the backend until an admin deletes the PV object","It is recycled with rm -rf and made Available for new claims","It is deleted with its backing storage, since StorageClass reclaimPolicy defaults to Delete","It stays Bound to the deleted PVC until the Pod is also deleted"],2,"StorageClasses default to reclaimPolicy Delete, so dynamically created volumes are cleaned up with their claims. Set reclaimPolicy: Retain on the class (or patch the PV) for data you must keep. Recycle is deprecated.","Configure volume types, access modes and reclaim policies"],["cka59",0,4,"A new PVC using StorageClass local-ssd stays Pending with the event \"waiting for first consumer to be created before binding\". What does it mean?",["The provisioner is broken and cannot create volumes","Expected: volumeBindingMode WaitForFirstConsumer delays binding until a Pod uses the PVC","The PVC's access mode does not match any PV","The namespace ResourceQuota for requests.storage has been reached, so binding is paused for now"],1,"WaitForFirstConsumer lets the scheduler pick a node first so the volume is created where the Pod can use it (important for local and zonal storage). Create the Pod and the PVC will bind.","Implement storage classes and dynamic volume provisioning"],["cka60",0,4,"PVCs without storageClassName stay Pending on a new kubeadm cluster, even though StorageClass nfs-client exists. What makes them use nfs-client automatically?",["Annotate nfs-client with storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: \"true\"","Set volumeBindingMode: Immediate on nfs-client","Label every namespace with storageclass=nfs-client","Rename the class to default so PVCs find it"],0,"PVCs with no class get the default StorageClass, which is chosen by that annotation. The class name default has no special meaning.","Implement storage classes and dynamic volume provisioning"],["cka61",0,4,"A 10Gi PVC must grow to 20Gi. Its StorageClass has allowVolumeExpansion: true. What do you change?",["spec.capacity.storage on the bound PV to 20Gi","The StorageClass parameters, then recreate the PVC","spec.resources.requests.storage on the PVC to 20Gi","The Pod's volume definition, adding sizeLimit: 20Gi"],2,"Expansion is requested on the PVC; the CSI driver resizes the volume and the PV follows. PVCs can only grow, not shrink, and only when the class allows expansion.","Manage persistent volumes and persistent volume claims"],["cka62",0,4,"Read these two objects:\n\nPV:  capacity 5Gi, accessModes [ReadWriteOnce], storageClassName: manual\nPVC: requests 10Gi, accessModes [ReadWriteOnce], storageClassName: manual\n\nThe PVC stays Pending. Why?",["The PV is smaller than the 10Gi the claim requests","The access modes must be ReadWriteMany to bind statically","storageClassName manual only works with dynamic provisioning","The PVC must name the PV in spec.volumeName to bind"],0,"A PV can bind only if its class and access modes match and its capacity is at least the request. A larger PV can satisfy a smaller claim, but not the other way round. volumeName is optional.","Manage persistent volumes and persistent volume claims"],["cka63",0,4,"A database volume must be writable by exactly one Pod in the whole cluster, even if a second replica is scheduled onto the same node. Which access mode enforces that?",["ReadWriteOnce","ReadOnlyMany","ReadWriteMany","ReadWriteOncePod"],3,"ReadWriteOnce limits mounting to one node, but several Pods on that node can still use it. ReadWriteOncePod (CSI volumes only) limits it to a single Pod.","Configure volume types, access modes and reclaim policies"],["cka64",0,4,"You create a PV of type local pointing at /mnt/disks/ssd1 on node worker2. The API server rejects it. What is required?",["A StorageClass with a local provisioner","spec.nodeAffinity that selects worker2","The hostPath type set to DirectoryOrCreate","A ReadWriteMany access mode for local disks"],1,"Local PVs must declare nodeAffinity so the scheduler knows which node holds the disk. There is no dynamic provisioner for local volumes in core Kubernetes, so a no-provisioner StorageClass is only optional.","Configure volume types, access modes and reclaim policies"],["cka65",0,4,"A StatefulSet with volumeClaimTemplates is scaled from 3 to 1 replica. What happens to data-db-1 and data-db-2 by default?",["They are deleted together with the Pods so the storage is freed","They are kept, so the data is still there if you scale back up","They are resized to zero and marked Released","They are bound to db-0 as extra volumes"],1,"By default StatefulSet PVCs are retained on scale-down and deletion (persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy whenScaled: Retain), so scaling back up reattaches the same data. You can change the policy to Delete.","Manage persistent volumes and persistent volume claims"],["cka66",0,5,"Node worker3 is NotReady. You SSH to it. Which commands should you run first?",["kubectl logs -n kube-system kubelet-worker3","kubeadm reset followed by kubeadm join","crictl rmi --prune and a node reboot","systemctl status kubelet and journalctl -u kubelet"],3,"NotReady usually means kubelet is not posting status. kubelet is a systemd service, not a Pod, so its logs are in the journal. Resetting or pruning images destroys evidence before you know the cause.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes"],["cka67",0,5,"journalctl -u kubelet on a worker shows:\n\nfailed to load Kubelet config file /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml: ... no such file or directory\n\nThe file is there as /var/lib/kubelet/config.yml. What is the fix?",["Make the file name match the --config path kubelet is started with, then restart kubelet","Run kubeadm upgrade apply on the worker so that the missing kubelet config file is regenerated","Delete the node object so it re-registers","Restart containerd so it rewrites the kubelet config"],0,"kubelet's systemd drop-in (10-kubeadm.conf) passes --config=/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml. Renaming the file (or fixing the flag), then systemctl daemon-reload and restart kubelet brings it back. upgrade apply runs only on a control plane.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes"],["cka68",0,5,"kubectl returns \"The connection to the server 10.0.0.10:6443 was refused\". On the control plane node, which command shows whether the API server container is running or crashing?",["systemctl status kube-apiserver","kubectl get pods -n kube-system","crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver","docker service ls kube-apiserver"],2,"On kubeadm clusters the API server is a static Pod run by containerd, not a systemd unit. With the API down, kubectl cannot help; crictl talks to the runtime directly, and crictl logs <id> shows why it exits.","Troubleshoot cluster components"],["cka69",0,5,"After you edited /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml, the API server never comes back and crictl ps -a shows no kube-apiserver container at all. Where do you look?",["kubectl describe pod kube-apiserver -n kube-system","/var/log/kube-apiserver.log on the node","kubectl get events -n kube-system --watch","journalctl -u kubelet, for errors parsing the static Pod manifest"],3,"If kubelet cannot parse the YAML it never creates a container, so there are no container logs; kubelet's journal reports the parse error. kubectl needs the API server that is down.","Troubleshoot cluster components"],["cka70",0,5,"New Pods stay Pending with no events at all, while existing Pods keep running. Which component should you check first?",["kube-proxy","CoreDNS","kube-scheduler","The CNI plugin"],2,"The scheduler writes FailedScheduling events when it cannot place a Pod; no events at all suggests it is not running. Check kube-scheduler-<node> in kube-system and its static Pod manifest.","Troubleshoot cluster components"],["cka71",0,5,"You scale Deployment web from 2 to 5. The Deployment shows 5 desired, but no new ReplicaSet Pods are created and no Pods are Pending. Which component is most likely down?",["kube-scheduler","kube-controller-manager","etcd","kubelet on the workers"],1,"The Deployment and ReplicaSet controllers inside kube-controller-manager create Pod objects. If none exist, nothing reached the scheduler; a scheduler failure would leave Pending Pods instead.","Troubleshoot cluster components"],["cka72",0,5,"A Pod stays Pending with this event:\n\n0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had untolerated taint {node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: }, 2 Insufficient memory.\n\nWhat is the most direct fix that keeps the control plane free of workloads?",["Lower the Pod's memory request or add memory to the workers","Remove the control plane taint so the Pod can run there","Add a toleration for the control plane taint to the Pod spec","Increase the Pod's memory limit so it gets more memory"],0,"The two workers lack allocatable memory for the request. Removing or tolerating the control plane taint would put workloads there. Scheduling uses requests, so raising the limit does not help.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes"],["cka73",0,5,"You must write the name of the Pod using the most CPU across all namespaces to /opt/top.txt. Which command lists them in that order?",["kubectl get pods -A --sort-by=.spec.cpu","kubectl describe nodes | grep -i cpu","kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu","kubectl top nodes --containers --sort-by=cpu"],2,"kubectl top pods supports --sort-by=cpu or memory with data from metrics-server. A Pod spec has no cpu field to sort on, and top nodes reports per node, not per Pod.","Monitor cluster and application resource usage"],["cka74",0,5,"You need to see how much CPU and memory has already been reserved by requests on node worker1. Which command shows this?",["kubectl describe node worker1 (Allocated resources section)","kubectl top node worker1","kubectl get node worker1 -o wide","kubectl get pods --field-selector spec.nodeName=worker1"],0,"describe node sums requests and limits of the Pods on the node against allocatable. kubectl top shows actual usage, which can be far from what the scheduler has reserved.","Monitor cluster and application resource usage"],["cka75",0,5,"An app writes its logs to /var/log/app/app.log inside the container, and kubectl logs shows nothing. What is the cleanest fix?",["Run kubectl logs with --previous to read the file","Mount /var/log/pods from the node into the container","Set terminationMessagePath to /var/log/app/app.log so kubelet copies the file into the log stream","Have the app log to stdout/stderr, or add a sidecar that streams the file to its own stdout"],3,"The container runtime only captures stdout and stderr; those streams are what kubectl logs and node log files contain. A streaming sidecar (for example tail -F on a shared emptyDir) exposes file-based logs.","Manage and evaluate container output streams"],["cka76",0,5,"The API server is down, and you need the recent logs of the etcd container on the control plane node. Which works?",["kubectl logs -n kube-system etcd-cp1 --previous","crictl logs <etcd-container-id>, or the files under /var/log/pods/kube-system_etcd-*","journalctl -u etcd on the control plane node","etcdctl logs --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379"],1,"Container logs are written by the runtime under /var/log/pods (and /var/log/containers links) and are readable with crictl even without the API server. kubeadm's etcd is not a systemd unit, and etcdctl has no logs command.","Manage and evaluate container output streams"],["cka77",0,5,"You need all Warning events in the cluster, newest last. Which command?",["kubectl describe events --all --type=Warning","kubectl get events -A --field-selector type=Warning --sort-by=.lastTimestamp","kubectl logs events -A | grep Warning","kubectl get events --warnings-only -o wide"],1,"Events support field selectors on type, reason and involvedObject, and --sort-by orders them. Events are API objects, not logs, and there is no --warnings-only flag.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes"],["cka78",0,5,"Node worker2 is NotReady and kubelet logs repeat \"container runtime network not ready\" plus \"failed to connect to /run/containerd/containerd.sock\". What should you check?",["The kube-proxy DaemonSet Pod on worker2","The CoreDNS Deployment in kube-system","The kubeadm bootstrap token for worker2","systemctl status containerd, then start or fix it"],3,"kubelet cannot reach the CRI socket, so the runtime is down or misconfigured. Once containerd is running, kubelet reconnects and the node returns to Ready.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes"],["cka79",0,5,"Right after kubeadm init, the control plane node is NotReady and the CoreDNS Pods are Pending. The API server and etcd are healthy. What is the usual cause?",["No CNI network plugin has been installed yet","The CoreDNS image cannot be pulled from the registry","kube-proxy has not been started on the node","The admin.conf kubeconfig has not been copied to ~/.kube"],0,"Until a CNI plugin (Calico, Cilium, Flannel) is installed, kubelet reports the network as not ready, the node stays NotReady and CoreDNS cannot start. kubeadm installs kube-proxy itself, and a missing kubeconfig would stop kubectl from working at all.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes"],["cka80",0,5,"Several Pods on node worker1 are stuck in ContainerCreating with \"failed to setup network for sandbox\". Pods on other nodes are fine. What is the likely area to investigate?",["The Service selectors of the stuck Pods","The ResourceQuota in the Pods' namespace","The CNI plugin Pod and its configuration in /etc/cni/net.d on worker1","The kube-scheduler's leader election lease"],2,"Sandbox network setup is done by the CNI plugin on that node. A crashed CNI agent, a missing binary in /opt/cni/bin or bad config in /etc/cni/net.d all cause this. Scheduling and quotas already passed.","Troubleshoot services and networking"],["cka81",0,5,"Pods on the same node can reach each other, but Pods on different nodes cannot. Services work only when the endpoint is on the local node. What is the most likely cause?",["A default-deny NetworkPolicy exists in the namespace and only allows traffic on the local node","CoreDNS is crash-looping and returning stale records","The Services use ClusterIP instead of NodePort","Node-to-node Pod traffic is blocked, such as a firewall dropping the CNI's encapsulation"],3,"Same-node success and cross-node failure points at the node-to-node path the CNI uses (for example VXLAN over UDP or BGP between nodes) being blocked or misrouted. A NetworkPolicy would block same-node traffic too.","Troubleshoot services and networking"],["cka82",0,5,"CoreDNS Pods are in CrashLoopBackOff and their logs show \"[FATAL] plugin/loop: Loop ... detected\". The nodes run systemd-resolved. What is the recommended fix?",["Delete the loop plugin line and leave forwarding as it is","Scale CoreDNS to one replica so it cannot query itself","Point kubelet's resolvConf at /run/systemd/resolve/resolv.conf, the real upstream list","Set dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst on the CoreDNS Deployment"],2,"systemd-resolved puts 127.0.0.53 in /etc/resolv.conf, so CoreDNS forwards queries back to itself. Giving kubelet the real upstream file breaks the loop. Removing the loop plugin hides the problem and leaves DNS broken.","Troubleshoot services and networking"],["cka83",0,5,"Which one-off command tests cluster DNS from inside the cluster and cleans up after itself?",["kubectl exec -n kube-system deploy/coredns -- dig kubernetes.default","kubectl run dnstest --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- nslookup kubernetes.default","kubectl debug node/worker1 -- nslookup kubernetes.default --cluster","kubectl get --raw /api/v1/dns?name=kubernetes.default"],1,"A temporary Pod uses the normal Pod DNS path, and --rm deletes it when you exit. The CoreDNS image has no shell or dig, and a node debug Pod uses the host's DNS by default.","Troubleshoot services and networking"],["cka84",0,5,"Pods on node worker4 cannot reach any ClusterIP Service, but they can reach Pod IPs directly. Pods on other nodes are fine. What should you check first?",["The kube-proxy Pod on worker4 (kubectl -n kube-system get pods -o wide -l k8s-app=kube-proxy)","The EndpointSlices of every affected Service","The CoreDNS ConfigMap in kube-system","The Ingress controller's logs"],0,"Service VIPs are implemented per node by kube-proxy; if its Pod on worker4 is down or crash-looping, only that node's Pods lose Service access. EndpointSlices are shared across nodes, so they cannot explain a single-node failure.","Troubleshoot services and networking"],["cka85",0,5,"Pods behind Service api are Running, but `kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=api` shows no ready endpoints. The selector matches the Pod labels. What should you check next?",["The kube-apiserver's --service-cluster-ip-range flag","The node's swap setting and kubelet cgroup driver","The Pods' readiness probes, since unready Pods are not served","Whether the Service type should be LoadBalancer"],2,"EndpointSlices mark Pods that are not Ready as not ready, so traffic skips them. A failing readiness probe (wrong port or path) is a common cause. The Service type does not affect endpoint readiness.","Troubleshoot services and networking"],["cka86",0,5,"kubelet on a node reports DiskPressure, and Pods are being evicted. What should you do first on the node?",["Free space, for example remove unused images with crictl rmi --prune and clear large logs","Remove the node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure taint with kubectl so that Pods can be scheduled there again","Restart the kube-scheduler so it ignores the condition","Add a toleration for disk-pressure to every Deployment"],0,"The condition and taint come from kubelet's eviction thresholds and will return until disk use drops. Freeing space on the node lets kubelet clear the condition itself; removing the taint by hand does not solve anything.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes"],["cka87",0,5,"Node worker5 lost power. Its Pods show Terminating for a long time, and a StatefulSet replica cannot be recreated elsewhere. After confirming the node is really off, what can you do?",["kubectl rollout restart statefulset <name>","kubectl cordon worker5 and wait for kubelet to confirm","kubectl scale statefulset <name> --replicas=0 --force","kubectl delete pod <name> --grace-period=0 --force"],3,"Without kubelet, the API server never gets confirmation that the containers stopped. Force deletion removes the Pod object so the StatefulSet can recreate it; only do this when you are sure the node is gone, or two copies may run. Deleting the Node object or adding an out-of-service taint are alternatives.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes"],["cka88",0,5,"You need every node's InternalIP on one line for a report. Which command prints them?",["kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=IP:.spec.podCIDR","kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.addresses[?(@.type==\"InternalIP\")].address}'","kubectl describe nodes | grep -i externalip","kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.internalIP}'"],1,"Node addresses are a list in status.addresses; the JSONPath filter picks the InternalIP entries. podCIDR is the Pod range, not the node address, and spec has no internalIP field.","Monitor cluster and application resource usage"],["cka89",0,5,"You are handed a kubeconfig with several clusters, and a task says to work on cluster k8s-prod. Which commands check and switch the target?",["kubectl cluster-info --context-list, then kubectl login k8s-prod to switch","kubectl config get-contexts, then kubectl config use-context k8s-prod","kubectl get clusters, then kubectl set cluster k8s-prod","kubeadm config view, then kubeadm config use k8s-prod"],1,"Contexts bind a cluster, user and namespace; use-context changes the current one in the kubeconfig. Running a task against the wrong context is a classic lost-marks mistake in the exam.","Troubleshoot clusters and nodes"],["cka90",0,5,"The API server log repeats \"connection refused\" when dialing 127.0.0.1:2379. Which command tells you whether etcd is healthy?",["kubectl get componentstatuses etcd-0 -o yaml","curl http://127.0.0.1:2379/healthz without TLS","systemctl status etcd on the control plane node","etcdctl endpoint health with --endpoints and the etcd --cacert, --cert and --key flags"],3,"etcdctl endpoint health (with TLS flags) checks each member directly; on kubeadm you also check the etcd static Pod with crictl. componentstatuses is deprecated and needs the API server, and kubeadm's etcd is not a systemd unit.","Troubleshoot cluster components"],["cka91",0,5,"metrics-server is Running but not Ready in a kind cluster, and its logs show \"x509: cannot validate certificate ... doesn't contain any IP SANs\" when scraping kubelets. What is the usual lab fix?",["Add --kubelet-insecure-tls to the metrics-server container args (lab clusters only)","Delete the kube-root-ca.crt ConfigMap so it is regenerated","Run kubeadm certs renew all inside the kind node","Switch metrics-server to scrape each kubelet's read-only port 10255 over plain HTTP instead"],0,"kind's kubelet serving certificates are self-signed without IP SANs, so metrics-server cannot verify them. Skipping verification is acceptable in a throwaway lab; in production enable serverTLSBootstrap and approve kubelet serving CSRs instead.","Monitor cluster and application resource usage"]]);
